---
import Term from "../Term.astro";
import Try from "../Try.astro";
import "./sternzeit.css";
---

<figure class="scene-frame wide" id="orbits">
    <div class="orbit-scene" data-export="orbits">
        <svg class="zdog" aria-hidden="true"></svg>
        <div class="orbit-labels" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <p class="vis-credit footnote">
            Drag to rotate. Visualized with <a href="https://zzz.dog/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zdog</a>.
        </p>
    </div>
    <label class="figure-slider" data-zoom title="From the Moon's orbit out to Earth's: drag, or turn the mouse wheel">
        <span class="note">Zoom</span>
        <span class="figure-slider-rail">
            <input type="range" data-field="zoom" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="0" aria-label="Zoom, from the Moon's orbit to Earth's" />
            <span class="figure-slider-scale" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="figure-slider-mark" style="left: 0%">Moon's orbit</span>
                <span class="figure-slider-mark" style="left: 100%">Earth's orbit</span>
            </span>
        </span>
        <output data-field="zoomValue" title="From the center of the frame to its nearer edge"></output>
    </label>
    <label class="figure-slider" data-offset="hours" title="The moment, a lunation either way from where it was set: drag, or turn the mouse wheel for an hour a notch">
        <span class="note">Time</span>
        <span class="figure-slider-rail">
            <input type="range" data-field="offset" min="-720" max="720" step="1" value="0" aria-label="Hours from the moment" />
            <span class="figure-slider-scale" aria-hidden="true">{["−30", "0", "+30"].map((mark, i) => <span class="figure-slider-mark" style={`left: ${i * 50}%`}>{mark}{i !== 1 && <span class="figure-slider-unit">{" d"}</span>}</span>)}</span>
        </span>
        <output data-field="offsetValue"></output>
    </label>
    <figcaption class="figure-caption">
        Earth's orbit around the Sun and the Moon's around Earth, seen from above the ecliptic: Earth's path over the
        year (faint), and dotted, its path over the coming <Term of="Lunation">lunation</Term> and the Moon's around it,
        with fainter still the Moon's own path along the Sun. Earth turns with the{" "}
        <Term of="Sidereal time">sidereal time</Term> about its tilted axis, the chosen place a dot on it with its
        zenith as a short line, and both are lit by the Sun where it stands. The crosses are the{" "}
        <Term of="Lagrange points">Lagrange points</Term>, with the lines they keep still on as Earth goes round, the
        equinoxes and solstices, the perihelion and the aphelion,
        and on the Moon's loop its <Term of="Apogee">apogee</Term> and <Term of="Perigee">perigee</Term> and its nodes,
        where it crosses the ecliptic (☊ northwards), each shown once it stands clear; the dotted ray from the Sun
        points to the vernal equinox. The zoom runs from the Moon's orbit out to
        Earth's, its readout the reach from the center of the frame to its nearer edge. Orbits to
        scale; the Sun,
        Earth and the Moon are not, and grow as the zoom brings them in.
        <Try label="A total lunar eclipse" set="date=2026-03-03T11:34Z" fig="orbits">
            the Moon in line behind Earth, full, in its shadow; zoom out, and Earth is a few weeks from the March equinox.
        </Try>
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
    import { offsetSliders } from "./offset";
    offsetSliders();
</script>

<script>
    import "./orbits";
</script>
